faq:
  - q: "What is Anthropic's hiring process?"
    a: "Anthropic's hiring process varies significantly by function. For research and engineering roles: recruiter screen → technical take-home or live coding → 4–6 loop interviews covering technical depth, research understanding, and values alignment. For policy and trust & safety: recruiter screen → writing sample or policy case → panel interviews. For operations and business roles: recruiter screen → skills-based assessments → structured behavioral panel. The process typically runs 4–8 weeks. Anthropic places heavy weight on mission alignment — candidates who cannot articulate a genuine interest in AI safety and responsible AI development are filtered early."
  - q: "What does Anthropic look for in candidates?"
    a: "Anthropic looks for three things above all else: (1) Mission alignment — a genuine belief in the importance of AI safety and responsible development, not just an interest in AI or Claude as a product. (2) Technical excellence in your domain — for research roles, a strong publication record or equivalent research depth; for engineering, distributed systems and ML infrastructure experience; for policy, deep expertise in AI governance or adjacent regulation. (3) Intellectual honesty — the culture prizes people who can hold uncertainty comfortably, update on evidence, and engage seriously with hard problems without overconfidence. Anthropic is skeptical of people who perform mission alignment without demonstrating substantive knowledge of the AI safety problem space."
  - q: "How do I get a research role at Anthropic?"
    a: "Anthropic's research team (Alignment Science, Interpretability, Model Welfare, Constitutional AI) is highly selective. Strong candidates have: a PhD from a top program (Stanford, MIT, Berkeley, CMU, Oxford, Cambridge) or equivalent self-directed research, at least 1–2 first-author publications in machine learning, AI safety, or interpretability, and demonstrated engagement with Anthropic's published research (the Constitutional AI paper, Mechanistic Interpretability work, Responsible Scaling Policy). Research applications without a publication record are rarely advanced. For candidates without a PhD, demonstrating independent research output — replications, novel experiments, or published analyses of existing models — can substitute."
  - q: "What engineering roles exist at Anthropic and what do they require?"
    a: "Anthropic has several engineering tracks: (1) Model training infrastructure — large-scale distributed systems, GPU cluster orchestration, TPU/GPU optimization. Deep experience with PyTorch, CUDA, and training efficiency is required. (2) Product engineering — building Claude's API, the Claude.ai interface, and Workspaces. Full-stack and backend experience with strong system design skills. (3) Safety engineering — building evaluation frameworks, red-teaming systems, and alignment tooling. Requires a blend of ML knowledge and software engineering rigor. (4) Research engineering — supporting alignment and interpretability research with scalable experiments. Requires ML experience and the ability to work closely with research scientists."
  - q: "What does Anthropic pay in 2026?"
    a: "Anthropic compensation is competitive with frontier AI labs and top-tier tech companies. Research Scientist: $250K–$450K+ TC depending on seniority and equity package. Senior/Staff Research Engineer: $220K–$400K TC. Senior Software Engineer: $200–350K TC. Policy roles: $150–250K TC. Equity is meaningful — Anthropic is one of the most heavily-funded private AI companies, and equity packages reflect this. Compensation has grown significantly with each major funding round. Anthropic's culture is mission-first; the people who thrive there are not primarily motivated by compensation, but the pay reflects the competitive AI talent market."
  - q: "Is it possible to get a job at Anthropic without a research background?"
    a: "Yes — the majority of Anthropic's employees are not researchers. Anthropic has substantial engineering, product, policy, legal, finance, recruiting, and operations teams. For non-research roles, the bar is genuine mission alignment (not just interest in AI) plus excellence in your domain. Product roles require strong product management experience with some AI product context. Operations and people roles require organizational excellence. Legal and policy roles require deep functional expertise plus credibility to work on AI governance questions. The differentiator across all roles is substantive engagement with what Anthropic is actually doing — reading the papers, understanding the RSP (Responsible Scaling Policy), and being able to speak coherently about why safe AI development matters."
