      <h2 class="section-title section-title-glow">Understanding Anthropic's mission and culture</h2>
      <p>Anthropic is an AI safety company, not an AI product company. The distinction matters for hiring. The founding team left OpenAI specifically over concerns about safe AI development — and that founding intent shapes every hiring decision.</p>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li><strong>Read the papers before applying.</strong> Anthropic's research is public: Constitutional AI, Mechanistic Interpretability, Sleeper Agents, the Responsible Scaling Policy (RSP). Candidates who apply without having engaged with this work are spotted immediately in interviews. Reading at least 3–4 key papers is the minimum bar for any role.</li>
        <li><strong>Know what "AI safety" actually means at Anthropic.</strong> It is not just content moderation or toxicity filtering. Anthropic's safety work covers alignment (making models do what we want), interpretability (understanding why models do what they do), and policy (ensuring development at scale is governed responsibly). Be able to discuss the distinction.</li>
        <li><strong>Mission alignment is tested, not assumed.</strong> Interviewers will directly ask why AI safety matters to you personally. Vague answers ("I think it's important") fail. Candidates who have read the alignment literature, engaged with EA or AI safety communities, or can articulate the specific risks they are most concerned about perform significantly better.</li>
        <li><strong>Intellectual humility is a hiring signal.</strong> Anthropic's culture actively penalizes overconfidence. Saying "I'm not sure" or "I'd need to think about that more carefully" is a strength, not a weakness — if followed by genuine engagement with the question.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="content-panel">
      <h2 class="section-title section-title-glow">Role-specific preparation by function</h2>
      <h3>Research Scientist / Research Engineer</h3>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li>Have a clear research agenda and be able to defend it: what do you think are the most important unsolved problems in AI safety or interpretability?</li>
        <li>Know Anthropic's published interpretability work deeply (superposition, features as linear combinations, circuits)</li>
        <li>Prepare to discuss your publications, experiments, or independent research projects at a technical depth that sustains 45+ minutes of probing questions</li>
      </ul>
      <h3 style="margin-top:20px;">Software Engineer (Product / Infrastructure)</h3>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li>System design at scale: Claude's API serves millions of calls — prepare for distributed systems, API design, and latency/reliability tradeoffs</li>
        <li>LeetCode medium/hard for coding rounds; clean, tested code with clear problem decomposition</li>
        <li>Be able to articulate how your engineering work connects to Anthropic's safety mission — product infra is not separate from the mission</li>
      </ul>
      <h3 style="margin-top:20px;">Policy / Trust & Safety</h3>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li>Engage seriously with the RSP, EU AI Act, Executive Order on AI, and NIST AI RMF — know the landscape</li>
        <li>Prepare a policy memo-style writing sample demonstrating clear analysis of an AI governance question</li>
        <li>Have opinions about tradeoffs in AI policy — not just descriptions of existing frameworks</li>
      </ul>
